WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.667
Two Inkos, one game, one speed setting.

2
00:00:03.667 --> 00:00:06.467
This one holds D and walks right.

3
00:00:06.467 --> 00:00:09.833
This one holds W and D
and walks diagonally.

4
00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:15.400
Same speed setting, so why has
the diagonal one gone 41% farther?

5
00:00:15.433 --> 00:00:17.333
The answer is vectors.

6
00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:20.600
Let's build them up, one piece at a time.

7
00:00:21.867 --> 00:00:27.300
A vector is a displacement:
how far, and in which direction.

8
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.567
Draw it as an arrow: the length is
its size, the head shows the way.

9
00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:36.233
Slide the same arrow anywhere,
and it's still the same vector,

10
00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:38.933
because where it starts isn't part of it.

11
00:00:38.933 --> 00:00:43.167
In code, a vector is
just numbers, x and y.

12
00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:48.300
Put its tail at the origin, and the
tip lands exactly on the point x, y.

13
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:54.300
Same numbers, different meaning: a point
is a fixed place, and a vector is a move.

14
00:00:54.700 --> 00:01:00.300
One special case: the zero vector has
length zero, and no direction at all.

15
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:01.867
Remember it.

16
00:01:02.733 --> 00:01:06.267
Adding vectors means walking
them one after another.

17
00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:10.133
Inko walks a, then walks
b from where a ended.

18
00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:14.433
Placed tip to tail, the sum is the
straight arrow from start to finish.

19
00:01:14.433 --> 00:01:19.633
Walk b first, then a,
and you land in the same place.

20
00:01:19.633 --> 00:01:23.600
The two paths make a parallelogram,
and the sum is its diagonal.

21
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.000
In numbers, add the parts.

22
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.033
Here a is the vector (3, 1).

23
00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:32.200
And b is the vector (−2, 4).

24
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.633
Together they give the vector (1, 5).

25
00:01:36.833 --> 00:01:39.300
Subtraction answers a game question:

26
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.333
which way should the enemy
move to reach the player?

27
00:01:42.333 --> 00:01:44.567
Take player − enemy.

28
00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:48.533
It's the vector from the enemy
to the player, tail at the enemy,

29
00:01:48.533 --> 00:01:52.967
tip on the player. Move along it,
and the slime closes in.

30
00:01:52.967 --> 00:01:57.300
Swap the order, enemy − player,
and the arrow points away.

31
00:01:57.333 --> 00:01:59.667
The slime turns its back and runs.

32
00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:02.933
The code still runs, so the bug is silent.

33
00:02:02.933 --> 00:02:06.100
Always take the target
minus your own position.

34
00:02:07.133 --> 00:02:11.833
Multiplying by a number scales a vector:
every part gets multiplied.

35
00:02:11.833 --> 00:02:14.733
Times two stretches it
along the same line.

36
00:02:14.733 --> 00:02:16.600
Times a half shrinks it.

37
00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:20.200
A negative number flips it a full 180°.

38
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.133
Take the vector (4, −2).

39
00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:28.167
Multiply it by −1.5,
and you get the vector (−6, 3):

40
00:02:28.167 --> 00:02:31.433
one and a half times as long,
pointing the other way.

41
00:02:31.433 --> 00:02:37.433
Add, subtract and scale: three tiny
functions behind most simple game motion.

42
00:02:38.867 --> 00:02:40.633
How long is a vector?

43
00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:45.633
Its x and y are the legs of a right
triangle, so Pythagoras answers:

44
00:02:45.633 --> 00:02:50.300
|v| = √(x² + y²).

45
00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:54.200
For (3, 4), that's the
root of nine plus sixteen,

46
00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.100
root twenty-five: five.

47
00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:01.267
In 3D, add z squared under the root.

48
00:03:01.267 --> 00:03:06.533
A handy trick: to compare two lengths,
compare the squared lengths instead.

49
00:03:06.533 --> 00:03:08.900
Same answer, with no square root,

50
00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:13.467
which costs more than a
multiply on the CPU and the GPU.

51
00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.567
Normalizing divides a
vector by its own length.

52
00:03:17.567 --> 00:03:22.667
What's left is a unit vector:
same direction, length exactly one,

53
00:03:22.667 --> 00:03:25.167
its tip on the circle of radius one.

54
00:03:25.167 --> 00:03:30.233
Back to the race. Holding
W adds the vector (0, 1).

55
00:03:30.233 --> 00:03:33.400
Holding D adds the vector (1, 0).

56
00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.600
Together: the vector (1, 1),

57
00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.033
and its length is √2, about 1.41.

58
00:03:39.033 --> 00:03:40.933
That's the forty-one percent.

59
00:03:40.933 --> 00:03:45.167
Normalize before multiplying by speed,
and both cover the same distance.

60
00:03:45.167 --> 00:03:51.167
One trap: the slime lands exactly on Inko,
and player − enemy is the zero vector.

61
00:03:51.533 --> 00:03:56.400
A hand-written normalize divides
zero by zero, giving NaN.

62
00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.233
That NaN spreads through every step,
and the slime just vanishes,

63
00:04:00.233 --> 00:04:02.300
with no error anywhere.

64
00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.433
So check the length before you divide,

65
00:04:04.433 --> 00:04:07.833
and the same goes for the
input when no key is held.

66
00:04:09.033 --> 00:04:12.633
The same x, y, z can play three roles.

67
00:04:12.633 --> 00:04:15.733
A position is a place,
measured from the origin.

68
00:04:15.733 --> 00:04:20.900
A direction only says which way, usually
with length one, and it has no position:

69
00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:25.333
slide a box, and its top
face's normal stays the same.

70
00:04:25.333 --> 00:04:28.467
A velocity is direction
and speed together:

71
00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:31.067
twice as long means twice as fast.

72
00:04:31.067 --> 00:04:33.667
And here's the heart of
the simplest game loop:

73
00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:38.333
every frame, position += velocity * dt.

74
00:04:38.333 --> 00:04:41.567
Just addition and scaling, with new names.

75
00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:46.600
So: a vector is a move,
an arrow or a tuple.

76
00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:50.467
Add tip to tail, subtract to
point from one place to another,

77
00:04:50.467 --> 00:04:56.300
scale to stretch or flip, Pythagoras for
length, and normalize for pure direction.

78
00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:02.300
Watch for the traps: diagonal speed,
subtraction order, and the zero vector.

79
00:05:02.567 --> 00:05:05.233
Now open the Vector Addition
demo in this lesson,

80
00:05:05.233 --> 00:05:08.400
and try to shrink the sum
arrow to almost nothing.

81
00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.267
See you next time!

82
00:05:10.767 --> 00:05:12.333
If this clicked, give it a like,

83
00:05:12.333 --> 00:05:15.100
and subscribe so you don't
miss the next lesson.

84
00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:16.733
See you there!
